==Problem==

In the figure below, choose point <imath>D</imath> on <imath>\overline{BC}</imath> so that <imath>\triangle ACD</imath> and <imath>\triangle ABD</imath> have equal perimeters. What is the area of <imath>\triangle ABD</imath>?

<asy>draw((0,0)--(4,0)--(0,3)--(0,0));
label("$A$", (0,0), SW);
label("$B$", (4,0), ESE);
label("$C$", (0, 3), N);
label("$3$", (0, 1.5), W);
label("$4$", (2, 0), S);
label("$5$", (2, 1.5), NE);</asy>

<imath>\textbf{(A) }\frac{3}{4}\qquad\textbf{(B) }\frac{3}{2}\qquad\textbf{(C) }2\qquad\textbf{(D) }\frac{12}{5}\qquad\textbf{(E) }\frac{5}{2}</imath>

==Solution 1==

We know that the perimeters of the two small triangles are <imath>3+CD+AD</imath> and <imath>4+BD+AD</imath>. Setting both equal and using <imath>BD+CD = 5</imath>, we have <imath>BD = 2</imath> and <imath>CD = 3</imath>. Now, we simply have to find the area of <imath>\triangle ABD</imath>. Since <imath>\frac{BD}{CD} = \frac{2}{3}</imath>, we must have <imath>\frac{[ABD]}{[ACD]} = 2/3</imath>. Combining this with the fact that <imath>[ABC] = [ABD] + [ACD] = \frac{3\cdot4}{2} = 6</imath>, we get <imath>[ABD] = \frac{2}{5}[ABC] = \frac{2}{5} \cdot 6 = \boxed{\textbf{(D) } \frac{12}{5}}</imath>.

==Solution 2==

Since <imath>\overline{AC}</imath> is <imath>1</imath> less than <imath>\overline{BC}</imath>, <imath>\overline{CD}</imath> must be <imath>1</imath> more than <imath>\overline{BD}</imath> to equate the perimeter. Hence, <imath>\overline{BD}+\overline{BD}+1=5</imath>, so <imath>\overline{BD}=2</imath>. Therefore, the area of <imath>\triangle ABD</imath> is <imath>\frac{(2)(4)(\sin B)}{2}=4(\frac{3}{5})=\boxed{\textbf{(D) } \frac{12}{5}}</imath>

~megaboy6679

==Solution 3 (Not Very Fast)==

Letting point <imath>A</imath> be <imath>(0,0)</imath> we get point <imath>B</imath> to be <imath>(4,0)</imath> and point <imath>C</imath> to be <imath>(0,3)</imath>. The line <imath>BC</imath> has a slope of <imath>-\frac{3}{4}</imath> and it has intercepts the y-axis at <imath>(0,3)</imath> so the equation for line <imath>BC</imath> is <imath>y=-\frac{3}{4}x+3</imath>. Using the same methods from Solution 1 and Solution 2 you can find out that <imath>CD</imath> is <imath>3</imath>. The equation of a circle centered at <imath>(0,3)</imath> with radius <imath>3</imath> is <imath>x^2+(y-3)^2=9</imath>. Plugging in <imath>y</imath> you get <imath>x^2+(-\frac{3}{4}x)^2=9</imath>. Simplifying it you get <imath>x^2+\frac{9}{16}x^2=9</imath>. Multiplying it my <imath>16</imath> and adding you get <imath>25x^2=144</imath> which becomes <imath>x=12/5</imath> after solving for the positive answer. You can then plug in <imath>x=12/5</imath> back into the slope intercept equation and you get <imath>y=-\frac{3}{4}(\frac{12}{5})+3</imath> which becomes <imath>y=6/5</imath>. Letting that be the height you can see that the base is <imath>4</imath> so the area is <imath>\frac{1}{2}(\frac{6}{5})(4)</imath>. Which becomes <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(D) } \frac{12}{5}}</imath>.

Note: This uses coordinate geometry which could be useful when you don't know how to solve a geometric problem

